function HTMLHelp04()
    DialogWindow.Help04 = [[<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"><head><title>Help</title></head> ]] .. Style00() .. [[
  <body><div class="layout">
    <div class="h3-lP">
      <label>Milling Menu Help</label>
    </div>
    <div>
      <div class="helpMillingimage"><img src="]] .. Project.AppPath .. [[/Help/Images/Milling_Defaults.png"></div>
      <div class="h2-lP">
        <label>Milling Defaults</label>
      </div>
      <div class="h1-help">
        <p>Use the Milling menu to set the default bit diameters, the part layout, the joint fit and the drawer style.
        The menu has four areas.</p>
        <p><strong>Bit Defaults</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li class="h1-help"><strong>Finger Bit Diameter, Profile Bit Diameter - </strong>The gadget uses these
              diameters for its calculations only when no bit is selected on the Main Menu.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Part Milling</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li class="h1-help"><strong>Part Placement Gap - </strong>The distance between the parts on the sheet. It
              cannot be zero. It must be more than the profile bit diameter.</li>
          <li class="h1-help"><strong>Dado Height - </strong>The thickness of the material from the bottom of the dado
              to the back face of the panel. It cannot be zero.</li>
          <li class="h1-help"><strong>Profile Tabs - </strong>When you tick this box, tabs hold each part in the sheet
              while the profile toolpath cuts it out. The tabs are on the two edges that have no finger joints.</li>
          <li class="h1-help"><strong>Tab Length - </strong>The length of each tab along the edge of the part.</li>
          <li class="h1-help"><strong>Tab Thickness - </strong>The height of each tab. The profile toolpath leaves this
              much material under the part.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="background-color: #FFF4CE; border: 1px solid #D9A400; padding: 6px 8px;"><strong>CAUTION:</strong>
        Dado Height is the material that <strong>stays</strong> under the dado. It is not the material that the bit
        removes. The gadget cuts each dado to a depth of panel thickness minus Dado Height.</p>
        <p><strong>Joint Fit</strong></p>
        <p>A clearance and an allowance are different. A clearance sets the fit of a joint. An allowance corrects a bit
        that cuts too narrow or too wide. Change an allowance only when you change or sharpen a bit.</p>
        <ul>
          <li class="h1-help"><strong>Finger Clearance - </strong>The fit of the finger joints. The joint has this much
              total clearance. A typical value is 0.004" (0.1 mm).</li>
          <li class="h1-help"><strong>Finger Allowance - </strong>The allowance for the finger toolpaths.</li>
          <li class="h1-help"><strong>Bottom Dado Clearance - </strong>The fit of the bottom panel in its dado. The
              bottom must move freely in its dado, so this clearance is larger. A typical value is 0.012" (0.3
              mm).</li>
          <li class="h1-help"><strong>Dado Allowance - </strong>The allowance for all dado toolpaths. One bit cuts all
              of the dadoes.</li>
          <li class="h1-help"><strong>Back Dado Clearance - </strong>The fit of the back panel in its dado. The back is
              glued, so this clearance is small. A typical value is 0.004" (0.1 mm).</li>
          <li class="h1-help"><strong>Profile Finish Allowance - </strong>When this value is more than zero, the gadget
              cuts each side panel and front panel in two passes. The first pass leaves this much material on each edge.
              The second pass cuts to the line at full depth. The bit bends when it goes from the air between the
              fingers into the material. The thin second pass decreases this error. Zero gives one pass. Try 0.010" to
              0.020" (0.25 mm to 0.5 mm). Set this value before you adjust the finger allowance.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>NOTE:</strong> An allowance applies to each side of a cut. A positive allowance leaves material. A
        negative allowance removes more material. An allowance changes the width of a dado by two times its value. It
        changes the fit of a finger joint by four times its value, because the notch and the finger both change.</p>
        <p><strong>NOTE:</strong> The gadget does not change the toolpaths that it made before. After you change an
        allowance, delete the toolpaths and run the gadget again.</p>
        <p><strong>Drawer Styling</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li class="h1-help"><strong>Joint Style - </strong>Dog Bone, Blind Dog Bone, T-Bone Joint, Blind T-Bone,
              Sniglet Joint or Blind Sniglet. Refer to the Definitions page.</li>
          <li class="h1-help"><strong>Blind Tab Thickness - </strong>For a blind joint style, the thickness of the
              material that covers the end grain.</li>
          <li class="h1-help"><strong>Bottom Style - </strong>Captive Bottom (Dado). This is the only bottom style.</li>
          <li class="h1-help"><strong>Bottom Dado Inset (Fixed) - </strong>The distance from the bottom edge of the
              panels to the bottom dado. The Blum specification sets this value: 13 mm (0.5"). You cannot change
              it.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div><hr /></div>
      <span class="helpimagesFooter"><img src="]] .. Project.AppPath ..
                              [[/Help/Images/Footer.png" width="80" height="20" alt="Page Footer"></span>
      </div>
      <div class="foot" id="FooterBox">
    <div><hr /></div>
      <div align="center">
        <input id="ButtonOK" class="FormButton" name="ButtonOK" type="button" value="Close"  />
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</body>
</html>]]
    return true
end -- Function End
--  ================================================================]]
function Style00()
    return [[<style>
	.FormButton {
		font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
		font-weight: bold;
		font-size: 12px;
		white-space: nowrap;
		background-color: #663300;
		color: #FFFFFF;
	}
  .h1-help {
    font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
    font-size: 14px;
    text-align: left;
    padding-right: 4px;
    padding-left: 4px;
  }
  .h2-lP {
    font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
    font-size: 16px;
    font-weight: bold;
    text-align: left;
    padding-top: 4px;
    padding-right: 8px;
    padding-left: 18px;
  }
  .h2-lP {
		font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
		font-size: 16px;
		font-weight: bold;
		text-align: left;
		padding-top: 8px;
		padding-right: 8px;
		padding-left: 8px;
	}
  .h3-lP {
		font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
		font-size: 20px;
		font-weight: bold;
		text-align: left;
    width: 650px;
		padding-top: 18px;
		padding-right: 8px;
		padding-left: 8px;
	}
	.layout {
		background-color: white;
		color: #000000;
		overflow: auto;
		width: 99.5%;
    height: 44em;
	}
	.pad-10 {
    padding-bottom: 2em;
	}
	.foot {
		position: absolute;
		bottom: 1em;
		width: 62em;
	}
	.helpTopimage {
    position: static;
		width: 550px;
    height: 40em;
		margin: 10px 10px 10px 120px;
	}
  .helpAboutimage {
    position: static;
		width: 550px;
    height: 45em;
		margin: 10px 10px 10px 120px;
	}
  .helpProjectimage {
    position: static;
		width: 550px;
    height: 20em;
		margin: 10px 10px 10px 120px;
	}
  .helpLayersimage {
    position: static;
		width: 550px;
    height: 35em;
		margin: 10px 10px 10px 120px;
	}
  .helpMillingimage {
    position: static;
		width: 550px;
    height: 28em;
		margin: 10px 10px 10px 120px;
	}
	.helpimages {
    top: 10px;
    float: right;
		margin: 10px 10px 10px 10px;
	}
	.helpimagesX {
    top: 10px;
    float: right;
		margin: 10px 120px 10px 120px;
	}
  .helpimagesFooter {
    float: right;
    margin: 10px 313px 1px 10px;
  }
	body {
		background-color: #3a4660;
		background-position: center;
		overflow: hidden;
		font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;
		font-size: 12px;
		color: #FFFFFF;
	 background-image: url(]] .. DialogWindow.myBackGround .. [[);
	}
	html {
		overflow: hidden
	}
</style>]]
end

